s and steels processed by controlled rolling. NB is directly linked to this category, establishing the principal material relationship in the community. [Data: Entities (122); Relationships (99)]\\n\\nThe source description lists several relevant steel classes, including C–Mn–Nb steels, Mn–Nb–V steel, low-carbon Mn–Mo–Nb steel, 0.03% Nb steel, 0.05C–Mn–Nb steel, Nb steel, and low-pearlite steels containing up to approximately 0.03% Nb. [Data: Entities (93)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Strength gains can involve a notch-toughness tradeoff\",\"explanation\":\"As-hot-rolled steel plates containing Nb are documented as having increased strength but reduced notch toughness when controlled rolling is not applied. This is the clearest performance limitation identified in the community. [Data: Entities (120); Relationships (97)]\\n\\nThe data distinguish controlled rolling as an important processing condition associated with Nb-bearing steels and indicate that it can address the toughness limitation described for as-hot-rolled material. The evidence supports a processing-dependent balance between strength and notch toughness, but does not provide quantitative performance values. [Data: Entities (120, 122)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Low niobium content is explicitly relevant to selected steel processing\",\"explanation\":\"A low-pearlite steel condition involving niobium content up to 0.03% is included in the community. The specified content is linked to selection of a rational processing scheme and is separately described as up to 0.03% Nb. [Data: Entities (1320); Relationships (1843)]\\n\\nThis provides a concrete composition boundary within the otherwise broader set of Nb-bearing steel applications. The supplied data do not establish that this limit applies to every Nb-bearing steel, so it should be treated as specific to the considered steel condition rather than a universal alloying limit. [Data: Entities (1320, 93)]\"}],\"rating\":6.5,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community has moderatel